{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChK76gZz4NoMyUd6JLYx4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the migration so it no longer crashes when a proxy model's permissions already exist under the proxy's content type, which is what happens when a model is recreated as a proxy. The fix is in `django/contrib/auth/migrations/0011_update_proxy_permissions.py`, and the full `auth_tests` suite passes (497 tests, 7 skipped). I didn't write a test that recreates the duplicate-permission case, so that path itself hasn't been exercised.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Duplicates no longer abort the migration:** each proxy model's permission update now runs in its own `transaction.atomic()` block. If it hits an `IntegrityError` because the permissions already exist, that model's update is rolled back and the migration continues with the next one.\n- **A warning is printed instead:** it names the old and new content types and the permission codenames involved, and asks the user to check all permissions for both models. In that case neither copy is merged or deleted, so the user needs to sort them out by hand.\n- **Database choice:** the update now runs on the database being migrated (`schema_editor.connection.alias`). The existing tests call the function with `schema_editor=None`, so when it's `None` it falls back to the default database."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":161,"cache_read_input_tokens":24331,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":161},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb3913f8-31f4-42b6-acc3-f7220e3fcd5e","uuid":"80aaf3c0-9a01-45d7-b77d-c3b83d94e057","timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:55:05.032Z","request_